<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ras al-Khaimah</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras">Ras</a> al-Khaimah is a port city on the northern coast of the United Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirates</a> and capital of the emirate of the same name. It became the main base of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qasimi">Al Qasimi</a> in the 18th century and was attacked by British expeditions in 1809 and 1819. The emirate joined the United Arab Emirates in February 1972, two months after its foundation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ras-al-khaimah.jpg" alt="Ras al-Khaimah" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stevenmccombe · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_view_of_RAK_City_from_Al_Qawasim_Corniche_flagpole.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The name Ras Al Khaimah means 'Headland of the Tent'. The Arabic word khaimah means tent, but it also refers to the palm frond or areesh houses that were common in the area. A creek divides the city into two parts, the old town in the west and Al Nakheel in the east.</p><p>The site has been continuously inhabited for 7,000 years, with finds dating back to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>. The city succeeded the port and trading hub of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julfar">Julfar</a>, which was a separate settlement with its own history and was abandoned in the early 17th century as Ras Al Khaimah rose. Julfar itself had a predecessor, Kush, in the suburbs to the north.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The navigator and cartographer Ibn Majid came from the town, and his writing suggests that it was already called Ras Al Khaimah, having eclipsed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julfar">Julfar</a> as the principal port of the Shimal coast. From 1507 the city came under Portuguese control. In the early 18th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qasimi">Qawasim</a>, the Al Qasimi, established themselves at Ras Al Khaimah and Sharjah and became a significant maritime force with holdings on both the Persian and Arabian coasts, often in conflict with British shipping.</p><p>After attacks on shipping the British mounted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> campaign of 1809, which largely destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qasimi">Al Qasimi</a> fleet; the expedition ended on 14 November with British casualties of five killed and 34 wounded. An arrangement of 1815 broke down, and in November 1819 a second expedition under Major-General William Keir Grant bombarded the town and stormed it on 9 December, finding it nearly deserted.</p><p>At Dhayah Fort, 398 men and some 400 women and children held out for three days before they surrendered on 22 December. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/general-maritime-treaty-of-1820">General Maritime Treaty of 1820</a>, which called for the end of piracy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, laid the foundation of the British protectorate over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trucial-states">Trucial States</a>, which lasted until 1 December 1971.</p><p>Ras Al Khaimah became fully independent of Sharjah in 1869. It was part of Sharjah again from September 1900 until 7 July 1921. Under Sheikh Saqr bin Mohammad Al Qasimi it was the last of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trucial-states">Trucial States</a> to join the United Arab Emirates, on 10 February 1972, following the Iranian seizure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-musa">Abu Musa</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greater-and-lesser-tunbs">Greater and Lesser Tunbs</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Ras Al Khaimah is an industrial port city and the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of Ras Al Khaimah, in the United Arab Emirates. Arabic is the language of the government-funded schools, and the city also has Indian schools and several colleges, among them the American University of Ras Al Khaimah and the Ras Al Khaimah Medical and Health Sciences University.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ras Al Khaimah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It became a stronghold of the Qawasim, or Al Qasimi family, the leaders of a seafaring tribal confederation whose main centre in the eighteenth century was <b>Ras al-Khaimah</b>, further up the coast.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sheikhdom-of-sharjah">Sheikhdom of Sharjah · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ras al-Khaimah</b>, which had held out for better terms and had just lost the Greater and Lesser Tunbs islands to Iran, joined on 10 February 1972.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-arab-emirates">United Arab Emirates · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Qawasim, an Arab tribe on the northern coast of the Oman peninsula, established themselves at <b>Ras al-Khaimah</b>, near the old port of Julfar, in the early 18th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/sheikhdom-of-ras-al-khaimah">Sheikhdom of Ras al-Khaimah</a></li><li><blockquote>After expeditions against the Qawasim of the Sheikhdom of <b>Ras al-Khaimah</b>, Britain imposed the General Maritime Treaty of 1820 on the rulers of the coast.</blockquote><a href="/state/trucial-states">Trucial States</a></li></ul></section>
